Nitrate Batuque

Ingredients: THRASH METAL + TROPICALIA + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Rhythm Acoustic Guitar
Creative Catalyst: Its originators were film projectionists scoring silent movies that never existed.

Nitrate Batuque is the sound of hyper-accelerated Brazilian acoustic agitation colliding with the crushing iron velocity of thrash metal and the corroded, synthetic smog of a dystopian sprawl. High-tension nylon and steel-string acoustic guitars execute percussive, syncopated rasgueado gallops at breakneck speed, locking into pummeling double-kick polyrhythms while jagged fuzz riffs, tropical horn stabs, and mechanical reel clatter simulate the frantic soundtrack to an unspooling film.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 185–225 BPM. A breathless, polyrhythmic collision where Afro-Brazilian partido-alto and samba-de-roda swing patterns are violently grafted onto the mechanical down-picking and double-bass drum onslaught of early thrash.
- Harmonic Palette: Dark Phrygian dominant and diminished metal progressions destabilized by sudden, luminous Tropicália chord inversions—sharp elevenths, minor-ninth suspensions, and bossa nova turnaround voicings played with razor-sharp attack.
- Production Signatures: Close-ribbon-miked rhythm acoustic guitars driven into blown-out tape saturation for a stinging, woody transient; spring-reverb-drenched psych-fuzz leads mimicking degraded 1968 television audio; rhythmic bed of mechanical optical-audio noise, projector motor hums, and subterranean industrial bass synthesis.
- Dynamics: Abrupt jump-cuts between hyper-speed acoustic-and-thrash walloping and eerie, suspended lulls where solitary nylon arpeggios hover inside room-reverb smog before exploding into double-time pandemonium.

Scene & Origin Lore

In the late 1990s, throughout the decaying industrial cinema belt of São Paulo's Brás district, graveyard-shift projectionists began holding illicit lock-ins inside shuttered Art Deco picture houses. Starved of live accompaniment for classic film prints they could never afford, they loaded blank, scratched, and heat-warped clear film leader into humming carbon-arc projectors, improvising deafening, live thrash-samba scores to the flickering dance of blank light and dust specks. Audiences—mostly factory night-shift laborers, rogue film archivists, and runaway squatters—gathered in grease-smeared tropical shirts pinned with rusted 35mm film sprockets. The ritual centered on the "Ghost Reel": thirty minutes of pure white screen accompanied by furiously hammered acoustic guitars, blazing tube amps, and modular drone boxes, treating the nonexistent cinema as an open wound to be stitched shut with sound.

Central Instruments

- Giannini Craviola & Percussive Nylon-String Guitars: Heavily battered acoustic guitars fitted with soundhole magnetic pickups, played with violent right-hand slap and flamenco-rooted thrash strums.
- Jackson / Charvel Electric Guitars: Loaded with high-output humbuckers, pushed through vintage silicon fuzz boxes and rotary speaker simulators for tropical-acid metal bite.
- The "Kineto-Percussion" Rig: Distorted cuícas, heavy surdo drums, and contact-miked 35mm film projector spools triggered alongside a hammered thrash drum kit.
- Korg MS-20 & Industrial Samplers: Supplying sulfurous dystopian sub-bass, decaying magnetic tape flutter, and air-raid siren modulations.

For Fans Of: Sepultura, Os Mutantes, Tom Zé, Ministry

Fictional Band Name: Os Carretéis Cegos (The Blind Spools)

Band Description: Formed in an abandoned booth above the old Cine Piratininga, Os Carretéis Cegos are anchored by lutherie dropout Tiago "Tasca" Silva, whose bloodied fingernails drive a reinforced Giannini nylon-string guitar with the speed of an automatic loom. Beside him stand bassist and synth technician Bianca Orsi, who runs patched telephone lines through an analog filter matrix, and drummer/projectionist Lucas "Graxa" Mendes, who plays a hybrid thrash kit augmented with foot-cranked film sprockets and an amplified, screeching cuíca. Their aesthetic pairs oil-stained overalls with vibrant, moth-eaten 1970s tropicalist silk collars, their instruments branded with reel-can serial numbers and burn marks from projector lamps.

